<section class="page-hero page-hero--compact">
  <p class="eyebrow">Mobile wallet</p>
  <h1>Install the Aegis ID wallet on Android.</h1>
  <p>This is a testing build, installed directly rather than through Google Play. It takes about two minutes and Android will ask you to confirm a few things along the way.</p>
</section>

<section class="band band--white">
  <div class="auth-grid auth-grid--compact">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Before you start</p>
      <h2>What you are installing.</h2>
      <p>
        The Aegis ID wallet holds the credentials your organization issues to you
        and approves sign-in and signature requests. It is signed by Vanguard
        Cloud Services, but it does not come from the Play Store, so Android
        treats it as an app from an unknown source and will warn you.
      </p>
      <p>
        Only install it if you were asked to by someone you know at your
        organization, and only from this page.
      </p>
      {{#if androidPackageName}}
        <p class="form-footnote">Package: <code>{{androidPackageName}}</code></p>
      {{/if}}
    </div>

    <div class="auth-panel">
      <div class="auth-panel__form">
        <h2>Download</h2>
        <p class="form-footnote">Open this page on the Android phone you want to install it on.</p>

        {{#if hasAndroidDownloadUrl}}
          <a class="button button--primary" href="{{androidDownloadUrl}}">Download the wallet (.apk)</a>
        {{else}}
          <p class="form-alert">A download is not published for this environment yet.</p>
        {{/if}}

        <ol class="install-steps">
          <li>
            <strong>Tap the download.</strong>
            Chrome will warn that this type of file can harm your device. That
            warning appears for every APK, not just this one. Choose
            <em>Download anyway</em>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Open the downloaded file.</strong>
            Android will say your browser is not allowed to install unknown
            apps. Tap <em>Settings</em>, turn on <em>Allow from this source</em>,
            then go back.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Install.</strong>
            Play Protect may say it does not recognise the developer, because the
            app has not been through Play review. Choose <em>Install anyway</em>
            if you were expecting this app.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Open the wallet and set it up.</strong>
            You will get a Wallet ID to give to your organization administrator,
            and ten recovery codes. Save the codes somewhere safe — they are shown
            once and they are how you get back in if you lose the phone.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <p class="form-footnote">
          Turn <em>Allow from this source</em> back off afterwards if you would
          rather not leave it enabled.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <h2>Updating and removing it</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="track-grid">
    <article class="track-card">
      <h3>Updates</h3>
      <p>
        Come back to this page and install the newer file over the top. Your
        Wallet ID, credentials and history are kept.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="track-card">
      <h3>Uninstalling</h3>
      <p>
        Removing the app deletes the wallet from the phone. The wallet itself
        still exists, but getting back in needs one of your recovery codes, so
        do not uninstall unless you have them.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="track-card">
      <h3>Trouble installing</h3>
      <p>
        If Android refuses with "app not installed", an older copy signed with a
        different key is probably still present. Uninstall it first — and expect
        to need a recovery code afterwards.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>
